{
  "teamId": 118,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Kansas City Royals were a team in the depths of a rebuild, finishing 59-103 and surrendering 869 runs — a painful reminder of how far the franchise had fallen from its 2015 World Series glory. From the opening weeks of April, the pattern was set: a promising moment here, a bullpen disaster there, and a rotation that simply couldn't hold leads long enough to matter. The month-by-month record told a story of a team that never truly found its footing, losing ground in April before briefly stabilizing and then collapsing again in August.\n\nThere were genuine bright spots scattered across the wreckage. Jorge Soler emerged as a legitimate power threat, launching home runs at a pace that hinted at the star he would eventually become. Hunter Dozier had a breakout campaign, delivering clutch hits including a grand slam that briefly made fans believe. Alex Gordon, the last remaining link to the championship era, continued to play with his trademark grit, providing emotional anchors in an otherwise forgettable season. Whit Merrifield remained the team's most reliable everyday player, always finding ways to contribute.\n\nBut the bullpen was a consistent disaster, with Brad Boxberger, Wily Peralta, and Ian Kennedy repeatedly surrendering pivotal home runs that swung games decisively away from Kansas City. The relievers allowed opponents to feast on high-leverage situations, turning close games into blowouts and giving the rotation no margin for error. August was the cruelest month, an 8-20 stretch that buried any lingering hope and accelerated the roster's transition toward youth.\n\nBy September, the Royals were playing loosely, mixing veterans with prospects and looking ahead to the future rather than the standings. Small victories like Ryan O'Hearn's solo shot and Cheslor Cuthbert's late home run provided fleeting moments of joy for a fanbase asked to be patient. The 2019 season was ultimately a necessary step in the rebuild — painful to endure, but full of data points about who might be part of the next contending Royals team.",
  "chapters": [
